  "purpose": "One contract, two implementations. The deterministic crisis assessment exists in the Next.js production path (frontend/src/lib/safety-assessment.ts) and in the FastAPI research path (backend/app/services/safety.py), which answers when NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is set. They carried the same version string and gave different answers (#374): FastAPI matched ASCII terms as substrings, so \"know\" raised imminence; it had no ambiguous tier, so 「もう限界」 was none; and its crisis card was English. Both test suites run every case here, hash their lexicons against lexicon_sha256, and FastAPI reads `responses` at import.",
  "notes": [
    "Expected values were generated by running the Next.js implementation, which is the one in production, and are compared exactly: risk_level, escalation_required, confidence and the ordered reasons.",
    "lexicon_sha256 is SHA-256 over one line per check, `name=spelling|spelling|…`, names and spellings sorted, spellings folded (NFKC, katakana to hiragana, lower case), lines joined with a newline. A term added to one side alone changes that side's digest and fails its suite; changing both means regenerating this value, which is the point.",
    "「今日、自分を傷つけてしまった」 is crisis because 今日 is an imminence term. That is the open false-positive question in #290; it is pinned here as the current behaviour of both sides, not as a ruling. Whatever #290 decides has to change both implementations and this row together.",
    "The vocabulary is a floor, not a ceiling. A `none` here means no listed term matched; it must never be used to hold back a model's own safety response."
  ],
